{
  "dom_id": "step-145542",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Absolute Value Inequalities Containing a Less Than (or Equal To) Symbol",
  "problem": "Solve the absolute value inequality $\\left\\vert\\dfrac{x-1}{6}\\right\\vert \\leq 1.$",
  "worked_solution": "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\dfrac{x-1}{6}$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $1.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\dfrac{x-1}{6} \\leq  1\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\frac{x-1}{6}\\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le x-1\\le 6 \\\\ -5 & \\le x\\le 7\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
